{"kind":"request","text":"Claude: decide the fate of 235 lines of uncommitted resident-memory work sitting in the gitseq-worktrees/resident-memory-500k worktree, and either land it under review or discard it with a reason. Evidence is assert 141fa6c9, the worktree survey hugh asked for on 2026-08-22.\n\nWHY IT IS AT RISK. The branch, request/resident-memory-500k at adda28c8, is an ancestor of main, so the lane looks finished from every ref-based view. Its commitment was cancelled. The uncommitted delta appears on no board and in no branch listing, and this working tree is the only copy. I nearly deleted the worktree this morning on exactly that reasoning and stopped only after diffing the tree instead of the ref.\n\nWHAT IS THERE. 235 insertions across internal/workroom/fold.go, internal/workroom/schema.go, internal/kernel/checkpoint.go and the tests for all three. Two distinct ideas.\n\nThe fold interns strings. A foldState.intern and internBytes canonicalize repeated text so a resident projection retains one immutable backing value per distinct string rather than one per occurrence, with a pooledString JSON adapter that lets encoding/json validate the payload while the fold adopts the unescaped bytes directly instead of copying them. The comment argues the projection stays byte-for-byte identical; that claim is the crux and is exactly what review should attack.\n\nThe checkpoint borrows rather than clones. appendBorrowedChunk compresses complete chunks straight from reader-owned event memory and clones only the bounded tail, on the stated reasoning that full chunks are encoded before the call returns while the tail is the only part that outlives it. Borrowing caller-owned bytes is the kind of optimization that is correct until an ownership assumption quietly changes, so the safety argument needs to be tested, not just read.\n\nThis goes to you because you carried the 500k projection and stress work and know what envelope this was chasing. If the measurements no longer justify the complexity, say so and discard it -- that is a satisfying answer, and a cancelled lane is decent evidence that the answer might be no.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT ASSERTING. I have not run the tests, benchmarked anything, or verified the ownership argument. I confirmed only that all five files differ from main's copies and that the branch is already in main. I am not committing it: the work is not mine and is unreviewed, and signing another actor's abandoned tree would attach my name to reasoning I have not checked.","body":{"area":"internal/workroom, internal/kernel","branch":"request/resident-memory-500k","conditions":"A report naming one of: (a) discard, with the reason -- superseded, unjustified by measurement, or unsound -- after which the worktree may be removed; or (b) carry, meaning the work moves to a clean branch off current main, the string-identity claim and the borrowed-chunk ownership argument each get a test that fails when the property is broken, go test -count=1 ./... and go test -race -count=1 ./... pass, artifacts are published at internal/workroom and internal/kernel, and it goes to independent review. Either outcome satisfies this request. Do not remove the worktree before the decision is recorded.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d","worktree":"/Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-worktrees/resident-memory-500k"}}